traceability.evidence:
Tests: `npm test -- --verbose` passes 38/38 suites and 290/290 tests, including all rules related to this story: - `tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts` - `tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts` - `tests/rules/require-story-annotation.test.ts` - `tests/rules/require-req-annotation.test.ts`
From the recorded test output: - Valid Annotation Format Rule (Story 005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION) - ✓ [REQ-IMPLEMENTS-PARSE] valid single @supports with one story and one requirement (default patterns) - ✓ [REQ-IMPLEMENTS-PARSE] valid multiple @supports lines with different stories and requirements - ✓ [REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT] valid mixed @story/@req/@supports usage in same block comment - invalid tests: - ✓ [REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE] @supports with no value is invalid - ✓ [REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE] @supports with only story path and no requirement IDs is invalid - ✓ [REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION] @supports with invalid story path format - ✓ [REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION] @supports with invalid requirement ID format - ✓ [REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION] @supports with multiple requirement IDs where one is invalid - Valid Req Reference Rule (Story 010.0-DEV-DEEP-VALIDATION) - valid: - ✓ [REQ-DEEP-IMPLEMENTS] single supports line with multiple requirements in multi-story fixture (see 010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT) - ✓ [REQ-DEEP-IMPLEMENTS] multi-story supports with shared requirement IDs (see 010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT) - invalid: - ✓ [REQ-DEEP-IMPLEMENTS] missing supports requirement in multi-story fixture (see 010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT) - ✓ [REQ-DEEP-IMPLEMENTS] disallow path traversal in supports story path (see 010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT) - Require Story Annotation Rule tests reference Story 010.2 and include: - a valid case with only @supports: `valid with only @implements annotation` (code uses `@supports ... REQ-ANNOTATION-REQUIRED`) - a BACKCOMPAT invalid case ensuring plain unannotated functions still error - Require Req Annotation Rule tests reference Story 010.2 and include: - valid case: `valid with only @implements annotation` (code uses `@supports ... REQ-ANNOTATION-REQUIRED`) - invalid case: `missing @req on function without JSDoc remains invalid under multi-story support` ,Core syntax + format support for @supports (`REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE`, `REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION`, `REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT`): - `src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts` - Detects @supports lines: ```ts const isImplements = /@supports\b/.test(normalized); if (isImplements) { const implementsValue = normalized.replace(/^@supports\b/, "").trim(); validateImplementsAnnotation(context, comment, implementsValue, options); return pending; } ``` This matches the story’s `@supports story-path REQ-ID1 REQ-ID2 ...` format and sends the value into a dedicated validator without interfering with @story/@req multiline handling. - JSDoc and create() comments explicitly reference Story `docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md` and requirements `REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE`, `REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION`, `REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT`. - `src/rules/helpers/valid-implements-utils.ts` - Implements the actual parsing and validation of @supports values: ```ts export const MIN_IMPLEMENTS_TOKENS = 2; // story path + at least one REQ
function parseImplementsTokens(...) { const value = rawValue?.trim() ?? ""; if (!value) { reportMissingImplementsValue(...); // no story path or req IDs return null; } const tokens = value.split(/\s+/); if (tokens.length < MIN_IMPLEMENTS_TOKENS) { reportMissingImplementsReqIds(...); // story path but no REQ IDs return null; } const [storyPath, ...reqIds] = tokens; return { storyPath, reqIds }; }
function validateImplementsTokens(...) { if (!options.storyPattern.test(storyPath)) { reportInvalidImplementsStoryPath(...); return; } for (const reqId of reqIds) { if (!options.reqPattern.test(reqId)) { reportInvalidImplementsReqId(...); // uses existing @req error builder } } }
export function validateImplementsAnnotationHelper(...) { const parsed = parseImplementsTokens(...); if (!parsed) return; validateImplementsTokens(...); } ``` - Error message helpers explicitly mention @supports and expected examples, satisfying format validation and error-context requirements from the story. - `src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-validators.ts` - Wraps the helper above as the public `validateImplementsAnnotation` used by the rule, with JSDoc tying it to Story 010.2 and `REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE`, `REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION`, `REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT`. - `tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts` - File header explicitly references Story 010.2 and `REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE`, `REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION`, `REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT`. - Valid cases: - `"[REQ-IMPLEMENTS-PARSE] valid single @supports with one story and one requirement (default patterns)"`: ```ts code: `/** * @supports docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md REQ-IMPLEMENTS-PARSE */`, ``` - `"[REQ-IMPLEMENTS-PARSE] valid multiple @supports lines with different stories and requirements"`. - `"[REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT] valid mixed @story/@req/@supports usage in same block comment"`. - Invalid cases validate all error conditions specified in the story for format validation of @supports. ,Deep validation of @supports requirements (`REQ-SUPPORTS-VALIDATE`, `REQ-ERROR-CONTEXT`, `REQ-SCOPED-IDS`): - `src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts` - Adds dedicated parsing for @supports: ```ts const IMPLEMENTS_TOKENS = { STORY_INDEX: 1, FIRST_REQ_INDEX: 2 } as const;
function parseImplementsLine(line: string): { storyPath: string; reqIds: string[] } | null { const parts = line.split(/\s+/); const storyPath = parts[IMPLEMENTS_TOKENS.STORY_INDEX]; const reqIds = parts.slice(IMPLEMENTS_TOKENS.FIRST_REQ_INDEX); if (!storyPath || reqIds.length === 0) { return null; } return { storyPath, reqIds }; } ``` - Validates each requirement ID against its own story file, with scoped caching: ```ts function validateImplementsLine({ comment, context, line, cwd, reqCache }) { const parsed = parseImplementsLine(line); if (!parsed) return; const { storyPath, reqIds } = parsed; const { reqSet } = resolveStoryAndRequirements({ comment, context, storyPath, cwd, reqCache }); if (!reqSet) return; for (const reqId of reqIds) { checkRequirementExists({ comment, context, reqId, storyPath, reqSet }); } } ``` - `checkRequirementExists` reports both the missing `reqId` and the `storyPath` in the `reqMissing` message, matching `REQ-ERROR-CONTEXT`: ```ts context.report({ node: comment as any, messageId: "reqMissing", data: { reqId, storyPath }, }); ``` - Requirements are cached per resolvedStoryPath (Map<string, Set>), so requirement IDs are scoped by story file, satisfying `REQ-SCOPED-IDS`. The docs and tests explicitly show reuse of the same ID in multiple stories. - `tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts` - Header ties to Story 010.0 and 010.2, and the test names call out multi-story `@supports` cases. - Valid tests demonstrate requirement validation and scoping for @supports: ```ts { name: "[REQ-DEEP-IMPLEMENTS] single supports line with multiple requirements in multi-story fixture (see 010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT)", code: `// @supports tests/fixtures/story_multi_a.md REQ-SHARED-ID REQ-ONLY-A`, }, { name: "[REQ-DEEP-IMPLEMENTS] multi-story supports with shared requirement IDs (see 010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT)", code: `// @supports tests/fixtures/story_multi_a.md REQ-SHARED-ID REQ-ONLY-A // @supports tests/fixtures/story_multi_b.md REQ-SHARED-ID REQ-ONLY-B`, }, ``` - The shared `REQ-SHARED-ID` in two different story files is accepted, confirming requirement IDs are only required to be unique within a story. - Invalid tests confirm error behavior and context: - Missing requirement in story file for supports: ```ts code: `// @supports tests/fixtures/story_multi_a.md REQ-NOT-IN-A`, errors: [{ messageId: "reqMissing", data: { reqId: "REQ-NOT-IN-A", storyPath: "tests/fixtures/story_multi_a.md" } }], ``` - Path traversal for supports story path is rejected with `invalidPath`. ,Rules accept @supports as satisfying story and requirement presence (`REQ-REQUIRE-ACCEPTS-SUPPORTS`, `REQ-BACKWARD-COMPAT`, `REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT`): - For story presence (`require-story-annotation`): - `src/rules/helpers/require-story-io.ts`: - `commentContainsStory` treats both `@story` and `@supports` as valid markers: ```ts function commentContainsStory(comment: any): boolean { if (typeof comment?.value !== "string") return false; return ( comment.value.includes("@story") || comment.value.includes("@supports") ); } ``` - `scanLinesForMarker` and `fallbackTextHasMarker` similarly accept both, so line scanning and fallback heuristics treat @supports as equivalent to @story for presence. - `tests/rules/require-story-annotation.test.ts`: - File header includes Story 010.2 and `REQ-REQUIRE-ACCEPTS-IMPLEMENTS`. - Valid case: ```ts { name: "[REQ-REQUIRE-ACCEPTS-IMPLEMENTS] valid with only @implements annotation", code: `/** * @supports docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md REQ-ANNOTATION-REQUIRED */ function implOnly() {}`, }, ``` The rule does not report a missing @story error here, proving @supports satisfies the story-annotation requirement. - Backward-compat invalid cases confirm that plain unannotated functions (no @supports) still error, preserving existing behavior. - For requirement presence (`require-req-annotation` and helpers): - `src/utils/reqAnnotationDetection.ts`: - All detection helpers treat `@supports` as equivalent to `@req` when deciding whether a function has requirement coverage: - `commentContainsReq`: `c.value.includes("@req") || c.value.includes("@supports")` - `linesBeforeHasReq`, `parentChainHasReq`, and `fallbackTextBeforeHasReq` all search for both markers. - `hasReqAnnotation` combines advanced heuristics plus direct JSDoc/comment checks, again treating @supports as satisfying requirement presence. - `src/utils/annotation-checker.ts` uses `hasReqAnnotation` to decide whether to report missing @req; this is invoked from `require-req-annotation`. - `tests/rules/require-req-annotation.test.ts`: - File header references Story 010.2 and `REQ-REQUIRE-ACCEPTS-IMPLEMENTS`. - Valid case confirming @supports is accepted: ```ts { name: "[REQ-REQUIRE-ACCEPTS-IMPLEMENTS] valid with only @implements annotation", code: `/** * @supports docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md REQ-ANNOTATION-REQUIRED */ function implOnly() {}`, }, ``` No `missingReq` error is expected or produced for this function. - Numerous invalid cases still verify that functions without any @req/@supports remain errors, ensuring backward compatibility. ,Error messages and context (`REQ-ERROR-CONTEXT`): - Syntax-level errors for @supports via `valid-annotation-format`: - `src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts` meta.messages: ```ts invalidImplementsFormat: "Invalid annotation format: {{details}}.", ``` - `src/rules/helpers/valid-implements-utils.ts` builds detailed `details` strings that include the problematic story path and examples, e.g.: ```ts details: `Invalid story path "${storyPath}" for @supports annotation. Expected a path like "${storyExample}".`, ``` - Tests in `tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts` assert these details for various failure scenarios. - Deep-validation errors for missing requirements via `valid-req-reference`: - `src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts` messages: ```ts reqMissing: "Requirement '{{reqId}}' not found in '{{storyPath}}'", invalidPath: "Invalid story path '{{storyPath}}'", ``` - `tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts` explicitly asserts that both `reqId` and `storyPath` are present in `errors[0].data`, including for `@supports`-based failures (missing requirements and invalid paths), matching the story’s requirement that error messages clearly indicate which story was checked. ,Backward compatibility and mixed usage (`REQ-BACKWARD-COMPAT`, `REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT`): - Existing `@story` + `@req` behavior remains fully supported and tested: - All pre-existing tests for `valid-annotation-format`, `valid-req-reference`, `require-story-annotation`, and `require-req-annotation` that rely only on `@story` and/or `@req` continue to pass. - `valid-annotation-format` still drives multiline story/req handling via `PendingAnnotation` and `finalizePendingAnnotation`; @supports handling is additive and does not alter this logic. - Mixed usage is explicitly supported and tested: - `tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts` includes: ```ts { name: "[REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT] valid mixed @story/@req/@supports usage in same block comment", code: `/** * @story docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md * @req REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT * @supports docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md REQ-IMPLEMENTS-PARSE REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT */`, }, ``` - `valid-req-reference` can happily process files that contain both `@story`/`@req` and `@supports` lines; tests cover mixed story fixtures and single-story vs multi-story usage. - No code path treats `@implements` specially at runtime; all parsing and validation look specifically for `@supports`. Existing textual uses of the word "implements" are in comments, test names, or legacy documentation only, consistent with ADR 011’s rename while preserving behavior. ,Documentation (`Documentation` acceptance criterion and story references): - `docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md` itself is present and matches the spec provided. - `docs/rules/valid-annotation-format.md`: - Clearly documents that the rule validates `@story`, `@req`, **and `@supports`** annotations. - Provides an explicit example: ```md - **`@supports` format support** - ... ```js /** * @supports docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md REQ-FOO REQ-BAR */ ``` ``` - `docs/rules/valid-req-reference.md`: - Documents that the rule enforces that `@req` and `@supports` reference existing requirements. - Explains how `@supports` works for multi-story deep validation and includes migration guidance showing before/after examples for moving from only `@story`/`@req` to `@supports`. - Explicitly notes requirement ID scoping: "Requirement IDs only need to be unique within a single story file; duplicates across different story files are allowed...". - README and rule list: - README’s rule summary mentions `traceability/require-test-traceability` and `traceability/prefer-implements-annotation` with wording that already reflects `@supports` as the target multi-story format. - Some older rule docs (e.g., `docs/rules/require-story-annotation.md`, `docs/rules/require-req-annotation.md`) still describe the multi-story tag as `@implements` in the narrative, but the working examples, tests, and implementation all use `@supports`, and the story’s own ADR note clarifies that ADR 010 is superseded by ADR 011. Functionally, the documentation for `@supports` is complete and present in the dedicated multi-story and deep-validation docs noted above. ,Traceability links from implementation to this story: - Multiple implementation files include `@story docs/stories/010.2-DEV-MULTI-STORY-SUPPORT.story.md` in JSDoc, mapping concrete code to this story and to specific requirements: - `src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts` (processCommentLine/processComment/create and messages) - `src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-validators.ts` (story/req validators, includes REQ-SUPPORTS-PARSE, REQ-FORMAT-VALIDATION, REQ-MIXED-SUPPORT) - `src/rules/helpers/valid-implements-utils.ts` (all @supports parsing and validation helpers) - `src/rules/valid-req-reference.ts` (token indices for @supports, parseImplementsLine, validateImplementsLine, and dispatcher in handleAnnotationLine) - `src/utils/reqAnnotationDetection.ts` and `src/rules/helpers/require-story-io.ts` (treat @supports as satisfying story/req presence checks) - Corresponding tests reference this story in their headers and/or test names: - `tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts` - `tests/rules/valid-req-reference.test.ts` - `tests/rules/require-story-annotation.test.ts` - `tests/rules/require-req-annotation.test.ts`
